WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.390
For those who predict

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:03.300
the American experiment can't last,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.580
and who worry the social
fabric is disintegrating

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.040
at a time of rising political division,

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.500
it's worth remembering that back

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.080
when the ink had barely
dried on the Constitution,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.360
the Founding Fathers
were deeply pessimistic

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.860
about the future of the
country they had created.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.267
Hamilton called the Constitution,

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.320
"A frail and worthless fabric."

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.070
Washington lamented the
growth of political factions.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.950
Adams thought a lack of civic
virtue doomed the Republic.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.610
Jefferson watched sectional divisions

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.430
between North and South with horror,

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.570
calling the sacrifices made by
the generation of '76 "useless

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.640
because it would be
thrown away by the unwise

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.837
and unworthy passions of their sons.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.677
"My only consolation," he wrote,

00:00:39.677 --> 00:00:40.510
"is to be that I live
not to weep over it."

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:41.343
Their pronouncements
seem almost histrionic

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.100
to the modern ear.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.390
In "Fears Of A Setting Sun,"

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.520
Syracuse University Professor
Dennis Rasmussen wrestles

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.890
with the Founders near universal pessimism

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.210
about the future of the
country they created.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.310
Partly I think that's because they thought

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.530
that so much was at stake.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.210
They really thought that the
future of Republican government

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.070
and the future of human liberty was riding

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.260
on this American experiment.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.450
And so the potential
failure of that experiment,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.100
they thought would be a
world historical calamity.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.500
I mean, they did have
really grandiose hopes.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.010
You read what they write,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.550
and they really think they're
coming up with a new system

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.740
that's gonna secure liberty for the future

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.360
that had never been done before.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.570
They're worried that what they think

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.140
of this world historical
achievement is going to fail.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.130
It's not just Washington,
Hamilton, Adams, and Jefferson-

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:33.351
virtually all of the Founders who lived

00:01:33.351 --> 00:01:36.290
into the 19th century became disillusioned

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.430
for one reason or another.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.790
Only one Founder stood
out as an exception.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:40.623
So, I'll confess,

00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:43.080
I assumed that Madison
would be disillusioned too.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.600
I initially set out to write a book

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.780
about five disillusioned Founders,

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.770
but really his final years were ones

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.480
of almost defiant optimism.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.930
Part of it is just temperament.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.740
He had a more even-keeled temperament

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.310
than the other Founders did.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.280
Part of it was that he just expected less

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.490
from politics than they did.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.630
He didn't have the sometimes
grandiose or utopian hopes

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.320
that some of the other Founders did.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.320
And one of them really, I think,

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.400
is just that he had lived so long.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.110
He's the one who outlives
all the other Founders.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:10.900
He lives until 1836.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.080
By the end of his life,

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.540
the country had weathered a
half centuries worth of storms.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.610
And he thought, well,
the longer it endures,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.760
the more durable it seems.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.790
Should Americans see
the Founders disillusion

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.460
as a sign that America
is flawed beyond hope?

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.710
After all, we are still beset

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.170
by many of the fears
the Founders identified.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.460
I think the fact that their
worries are still with us,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.150
but that the country is still with us,

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:33.980
the Constitution is still with us,

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.590
you could take in either
of two directions.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.360
On the one hand, you could say,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.880
well, the country has lasted this long

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:40.910
despite these problems.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:42.640
It's probably, as Madison suggested,

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.240
going to last a good deal longer.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.830
We can live with these problems in a way

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.160
that we sometimes don't
think we can, right?

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.900
We hear people pronounce the end

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.620
of American democracy at every turn.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.070
And the fact that it hasn't ended

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.970
in the past 230 years suggests

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:57.803
that maybe Madison was right

00:02:57.803 --> 00:02:59.840
and it'll last a good deal longer.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:00.860
On the other hand,

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.470
these problems probably aren't going away.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.660
We sometimes seem to think
that with the right tweak

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.710
to the political system,
if we just, you know,

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.920
get rid of the electoral
college or the filibuster

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.010
or whatever the pet worry
is, then all will be right.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.940
Then American politics will be fixed.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.910
But the fact that these
problems have been with us

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.630
since the very outset, since
the Founders themselves,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.140
suggests that, you know,
they might be more systemic,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.601
more baked in than we
sometimes dare to hope.

